  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "nettrace: Don't preallocate an INT_MAX-sized buffer",
  "description": "nettrace_3gpp_32_423_file_open() reserved RINGBUFFER_START_SIZE ==\nINT_MAX for the streaming buffer. g_array_sized_new() rounds that up to\nthe next power of two, so it is really a 2 GiB g_realloc(), for a buffer\nthat read_until() then fills 1024 bytes at a time. glib treats an\nallocation failure as fatal, so where the request cannot be satisfied\nthe process aborts during file open, before any packet is parsed. Any\nXML file with a <traceCollecFile> root reaches this.\n\nReserve 64 KiB instead and let the append path grow the buffer.\n\nImpact on 64-bit systems is low: the mapping is lazy, so the untouched\n2 GiB never becomes resident, and such machines generally have well over\n2 GiB of RAM anyway. It matters on 32-bit builds and under an address\nspace limit (RLIMIT_AS, cgroups) or without overcommit, where the\nallocation genuinely fails and glib aborts. It was also a waste for\nlegitimate traces, as the reservation is never released.\n\nThanks to Aisle Research for the report.\n\nFixes #21507",
